'''''Scrophularia auriculata''''', the '''shoreline figwort'''<ref>{{PLANTS|id=SCAU7|taxon=Scrophularia aurticulata|accessdate=8 November 2015}}</ref> or '''water figwort''', is a perennial plant of the genus [[Scrophularia]] in the family [[Scrophulariaceae]]. It is found commonly in Western Europe and North Africa, on the margins of rivers, ponds and similar damp places.<ref name="wildflow">{{Citation| url =http://www.wildflowersofireland.net/plant_detail.php?id_flower=407&Wildflower=Figwort,%20Water| title =Water Figwort| publisher =Wild Flowers of Ireland| access-date =28 June 2012}}</ref><ref name ="plantnames">{{cite book |title=Guide to Plant Names |last=Coombes |first=Allen J |year=1992 |publisher=Octopus Illustrated Publishing |location=London |isbn=9780600575450 |page=179 }}</ref>
It is an upright plant reaching 70&nbsp;cm with blunt oval, [[crenate]] leaves in alternate pairs on the greenish–purple square stem, most leaves may have two small lobes at their base. The spikes of flowers are held stiffly on square stems which arise from the main stem in the angle of the leaf stalks. The square stems have a wing running down each corner. These wings are more obvious than on the closely related common figwort (''[[Scrophularia nodosa]]''). The flowers are small, maroon-brownish and globular with two small lips above and below. The five sepals are green with a white margin, broader on the water figwort than the common figwort. The plant flowers from June to September (in the northern hemisphere) after when the flowers produce small spherical to pear-shaped capsules containing a large number of seeds.<ref name="wildflow"/>

The name ''auriculata'' derives from the [[Auricle (botany)|auricles]] at the base of the leaves.<ref name ="plantnames"/>

The water figwort is host to the [[Tenthredo scrophulariae|figwort sawfly]] whose larvae feed on the leaves.<ref>{{Citation| url = http://www.naturespot.org.uk/species/figwort-sawfly| title = Figwort Sawfly| publisher= www.naturespot.org.uk| access-date =28 June 2012}}</ref>

